Keep the seek-hls input fixture independent of the build host's CPU count.

The direct generator introduced by FFmpeg f699b3a8f52c151f61318b757abf04cab0811724
bypasses fate-run.sh's encoder thread setting. The CLI selects automatic slice
threads even with bitexact/int DCT; a three-core host uses four slice contexts,
whereas hosts with at least five cores use all five macroblock rows at 72px.
MPEG-2 motion predictors differ across slice boundaries.

Use the original reference fixture's five contexts explicitly. This preserves
all existing seek return, stream, timestamp, position and size expectations;
no codec source, reference output or check selection is changed. Actual pinned
9.0.2 ffmpeg/ffprobe measurements: CPUcount3 auto produces size3904 at DTS4.8 /
PTS5.1; explicit5 produces3860 and the full60-packet table is identical to the
original large-core auto fixture. Explicit1 would not preserve that fixture.

Sources: FFmpeg n9.0.2 tests/fate/seek.mak, fftools/ffmpeg_mux_init.c,
libavcodec/pthread_slice.c, mpegvideo.c and motion_est.c. This bounded test-input
patch is local; no matching official upstream fix was found.

--- a/tests/fate/seek.mak
+++ b/tests/fate/seek.mak
@@ -207,1 +207,1 @@
-        -map 0:v -map 1:a -c:v mpeg2video -bf 2 -g 15 -dct int -idct int -flags +bitexact -c:a mp2fixed \
+        -map 0:v -map 1:a -c:v mpeg2video -threads:v 5 -bf 2 -g 15 -dct int -idct int -flags +bitexact -c:a mp2fixed \
